  "query_preview": "..\n\nSE MI continues to reside on the northern edge of Ohio Valley high \npressure today maintaining light winds and limited cloud cover. With \nno change in airmass, temps will be similar to yesterday with highs \ntopping out in the upper 60s to near 70. \n\nUpper level pattern shift begins by this evening with ridging across \nthe central CONUS fully expanding over the Great Lakes as the \nsurface high shifts towards the Northeastern seaboard. As this \noccurs, a weak mid-level shortwave riding the warm front lifting out \nof the Midwest is progged to track into the western, and eventually \ncentral, Great Lakes. Initially this wave is supported by \nstrengthening low level jet pushing out of the Plains into the upper \nMidwest resulting in a modest area of showers while over IA/WI/IL. \nHowever with the LLJ fixed west of Lake Michigan, overall synoptic \nsupport lessens as it reaches lower MI likely leading to a steady \ndiminishing shower coverage trend as it crosses west MI tonight. \nThat said, can't completely write off light shower chances late \ntonight-early Saturday morning as the warm frontal boundary draws a \nrespectable ribbon of theta-e overhead. As such, have added entry-\nlevel slight chance/isolated PoPs (15%). \n\nRegion is firmly entrenched within low/mid level southwesterly flow \ndaytime Saturday with strong warm advection pushing 850mb temps into \nthe mid-teens C resulting in highs reaching the mid to upper 70s. \nCore of this airmass (upper teen C 850mb temps) is still favored to \nbe directed over the far western Great Lakes/upper Midwest reducing \nchances areas within SE MI manage to reach 80. Remnants of Isaias \nreach the Ohio Valley Sunday before being shunted towards the mid-\nAtlantic Monday. Despite this track well to our south, model \nsolutions continue to advertise the northern edge of the associated \nrain shield clipping portions of the area. Greatest rain chances \nfavored over the southeastern/easternmost areas (ie counties along \nthe Huron-Erie corridor) with potential QPF hinging on the extent \nmoderate showers are able push out of Ohio before the low starts to \neastward drift.",
